sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-47> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Almost any general feature proposed to characterize life, including self-organization, self-reproduction and complexity, occurs even in many systems with very simple rules, so life can only be recognized by specific shared details with life on Earth.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Proposed general features of life occur in many simple-rule systems";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Intelligence in the Universe” (from p. 822), p. 825. Scope: definitions of life, terrestrial and extraterrestrial"
}
